WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.819
A shared document can authorize access by
consulting a list: the guard identifies the

00:00:04.819 --> 00:00:09.978
requester and checks whether that identity may
read the document. A capability-style design

00:00:09.978 --> 00:00:15.194
instead gives a principal an unforgeable
reference carrying narrowly defined authority,
such

00:00:15.194 --> 00:00:18.256
as permission to download one object until a
deadline.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:24.074
Presenting the valid capability proves that
authority under the scheme's rules. Draw where

00:00:24.074 --> 00:00:29.524
the policy lives in each case. With a list,
changing one central entry can affect future

00:00:29.524 --> 00:00:34.912
checks. With a copied ticket, revocation may
require short expiry, an online revocation

00:00:34.912 --> 00:00:37.204
check, or another explicit mechanism.

00:00:37.204 --> 00:00:42.599
Neither design is automatically safer; the
choice depends on delegation, sharing, and how

00:00:42.599 --> 00:00:45.266
quickly permission changes must take effect.

00:00:45.266 --> 00:00:50.286
Suppose an export service must let a browser
download one generated report. Giving the

00:00:50.286 --> 00:00:55.364
browser the service's storage administrator
credential would expose unrelated files and

00:00:55.364 --> 00:01:00.676
future operations. Instead issue authority
limited to that report, the download action, and

00:01:00.676 --> 00:01:02.077
a short validity period.

00:01:02.266 --> 00:01:07.559
If the user shares that ticket, the recipient
may gain the same limited authority, so this

00:01:07.559 --> 00:01:12.617
sharing behavior must be intentional. Contrast
this with mandatory labels: a centrally

00:01:12.617 --> 00:01:17.558
defined policy can prevent a document owner from
granting access outside an approved

00:01:17.558 --> 00:01:18.969
classification boundary.

00:01:18.969 --> 00:01:24.248
A hybrid can authenticate the user against
organizational policy and then issue a narrow

00:01:24.248 --> 00:01:29.707
capability for delivery. Trace who can create,
copy, inspect, and revoke authority at every

00:01:29.707 --> 00:01:31.087
stage of that workflow.

00:01:31.087 --> 00:01:36.189
Imagine a valid approval message for
transferring ten dollars. An attacker captures
it and

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:41.405
submits it twice. The signature or
authentication code may still verify because the
contents

00:01:41.405 --> 00:01:46.281
are genuine; that check alone does not establish
that this is a new authorized action.

00:01:46.281 --> 00:01:51.498
Include an operation identifier, bind the
approval to its intended recipient and purpose,

00:01:51.498 --> 00:01:56.890
and define whether repeated delivery returns the
same result or creates another transfer. An

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:02.048
expiry limits the usable time window but does
not by itself prevent two uses within that

00:02:02.048 --> 00:02:02.459
window.

00:02:02.459 --> 00:02:07.562
Freshness, origin, integrity, and authority are
separate claims. The protocol must state

00:02:07.562 --> 00:02:12.898
which evidence supports each claim rather than
using the word authenticated as shorthand for

00:02:12.898 --> 00:02:13.594
all of them.

00:02:13.594 --> 00:02:18.812
A login challenge can be sent through an already
registered channel to test control of that

00:02:18.812 --> 00:02:23.915
channel. The challenge must be unpredictable,
bound to the relevant attempt, and accepted

00:02:23.915 --> 00:02:29.018
under explicit expiry and reuse rules. If an
attacker controls the recovery mailbox, that

00:02:29.018 --> 00:02:32.802
control may undermine the protection expected
from the login flow.

00:02:32.802 --> 00:02:38.169
Likewise, a service's good reputation is
evidence about past behavior, not a credential

00:02:38.169 --> 00:02:43.535
authorizing a particular action today. In a
collaborative marketplace, reputation might

00:02:43.535 --> 00:02:48.902
determine how urgently a transaction is
reviewed, while access to private records still

00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:50.752
follows an enforceable policy.

00:02:50.802 --> 00:02:56.308
Separate confidence in a participant from
validation of this message, this account, and
this

00:02:56.308 --> 00:03:01.454
requested effect. Otherwise a favorable history
can become an accidental bypass around

00:03:01.454 --> 00:03:02.531
concrete controls.

00:03:02.531 --> 00:03:08.015
A browser renders pages supplied by parties the
user does not control. Suppose a malicious

00:03:08.015 --> 00:03:13.377
page exploits a renderer bug. If the renderer
runs with broad access to local files, the

00:03:13.377 --> 00:03:18.862
exploit can immediately become a data theft
problem. A restricted renderer process changes

00:03:18.862 --> 00:03:24.041
that consequence: privileged operations must
cross a boundary to a component that can

00:03:24.041 --> 00:03:24.955
enforce policy.

00:03:25.031 --> 00:03:29.929
Draw the boundary between rendering content and
accessing sensitive operating-system

00:03:29.929 --> 00:03:35.176
resources, then label the allowed messages. The
design assumes defects may exist and tries

00:03:35.176 --> 00:03:40.424
to limit what a successful exploit can reach. It
does not make malicious content harmless,

00:03:40.424 --> 00:03:43.747
nor does it prove the boundary implementation is
correct.

00:03:43.747 --> 00:03:48.829
Evaluate both the compromise and the attempted
next step beyond the compromised component.

00:03:48.829 --> 00:03:53.883
Once a vulnerability is known, a patch can
remove the specific defect while isolation

00:03:53.883 --> 00:03:59.176
continues to limit damage from other defects.
The patch must reach users, so distribution

00:03:59.176 --> 00:04:03.041
and adoption time matter as much as producing a
corrected binary.

00:04:03.041 --> 00:04:07.937
Regression checks help establish that the fix
works without breaking expected behavior.

00:04:07.937 --> 00:04:13.115
Dangerous-site warnings address a different
point in the chain by reducing exposure to known

00:04:13.115 --> 00:04:18.067
hostile destinations. Draw prevention,
containment, and repair as separate stages
rather

00:04:18.067 --> 00:04:20.712
than treating them as interchangeable controls.

00:04:20.712 --> 00:04:25.562
The detailed browser process topology changes
across versions, so use the historical

00:04:25.562 --> 00:04:30.875
architecture as an example of reasoning about
privilege boundaries. The enduring question is

00:04:30.875 --> 00:04:33.878
what the attacker can do after one layer has
failed.
